  <page>
    <title>Walloon Brabant</title>
    <id>80898</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40734996</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-22T17:42:12Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>QuartierLatin1968</username>
        <id>93392</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>site web</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Belgian Province |
  EnglishName           =  Walloon Brabant|
  LocalNameInLanguage1  =  ([[French_language|French]]:Brabant Wallon)|
  LocalNameInLanguage2  =  ([[Dutch_language|Dutch]]:Waals Brabant)|
  LocalNameInLanguage3  =  |
  flag              = Image:Walloon Brabant Flag.png |
  coatofarms        = Image:Armes province brabant wallon.png|
  capital           = [[Wavre]] |
  area              = 1,093 |
  population_as_of  = (1999)|
  population        = 347,423|
  populationdensity = 318|
  map               = [[Image:BelgiumWalloonBrabant.png|220px]] |
  officialsite      = http://www.brabantwallon.be/ |
}}


'''Walloon Brabant''' is a province of [[Wallonia]] and of [[Belgium]]. It borders on (clockwise from the North) the Belgian provinces of [[Flemish Brabant]], [[Liege (province)|Liège]], [[Namur (province)|Namur]] and [[Hainaut]]. Its capital is [[Wavre]]. It has an area of 1,093 km&amp;sup2; and contains only one administrative district (''arrondissement'' in [[French language|French]]) with 27 municipalities.

Walloon Brabant was created in [[1995]] when the former province of Brabant was split into three parts: two new provinces, Walloon Brabant and Flemish Brabant; and the [[Brussels]] Capital Region, which no longer belongs to any province. The split was made to accommodate the eventual division of Belgium in three regions ([[Flanders]], [[Wallonia]] and Brussels).

The provincial population was recorded at 347,423 in January 1999, giving a population density of 318 inhabitants / km&amp;sup2;

The history of Brabant is at [[Brabant]]; see also [[Duke of Brabant]].

