  <page>
    <title>Battleground (1949 film)</title>
    <id>80916</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34875881</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-12T10:35:33Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Steve Eifert</username>
        <id>203230</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Battleground.jpg|thumb|Movie poster reprint of ''Battleground'']]
'''''Battleground''''' is a [[1949]] war film which tells the story of a squad of the [[101st Airborne Division]] trying to cope during the [[Battle of the Bulge]] at [[Bastogne]], [[Belgium]].  It stars [[Van Johnson]], [[John Hodiak]], [[Ricardo Montalban]], [[George Murphy]], [[Marshall Thompson]], [[Jerome Courtland]], [[Don Taylor]], [[Bruce Cowling]], [[James Whitmore]], [[Douglas Fowley]] and [[Leon Ames]].

The movie was written by [[Robert Pirosh]] and directed by [[William A. Wellman]].

It won [[Academy Awards]] for [[Academy Award for Best Cinematography|Best Cinematography, Black-and-White]] ([[Paul C. Vogel]]), and [[Academy Award for Writing Original Screenplay|Best Writing, Story and Screenplay]].  It was nominated for [[Academy Award for Best Supporting Actor|Best Actor in a Supporting Role]] (James Whitmore), [[Academy Award for Directing|Best Director]], [[Academy Award for Film Editing|Best Film Editing]] and [[Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]].

This film was notable for being the first post-World War II film to portray American soldiers as vulnerable and human, as opposed to inspirational and gung-ho.  While there is no question concerning the courage and steadfastedness of the members of the squad, they each have at least one moment in the film where they seriously consider running away, or scheme to get sent away from the front line, or vocally complain about being in the situation they are in.  In spite of all their fears and misery, though, they stay at their posts when the battle is at its worst, and exhibit great pride in their unit and in themselves.  A refreshingly realisitic look at the &quot;Greatest Generation&quot;, very close in time to the events portrayed.

==External links==
*{{imdb title | id=0041163 | title=Battleground}}

[[Category:1949 films]]
[[Category:World War II films]]
[[Category:Best Picture Oscar Nominee]]
[[Category:Best Supporting Actor Oscar Nominee (film)]] &lt;!-- James Whitmore --&gt;</text>
    </revision>
