      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">:''For the Coca-Cola energy drink, see [[Full Throttle (drink)|Full Throttle]].''
{{Infobox CVG| title = Full Throttle
|image = [[Image:Full_Throttle_Cover.jpg|250px|center|Full Throttle cover art featuring Ben]]
|developer = [[LucasArts]]
|publisher = [[LucasArts]]
|designer = [[Tim Schafer]]
|engine = [[SCUMM]] v7, [[INSANE (engine)|INSANE]]
|released = [[May]], [[1995]]
|genre = [[Adventure game|Adventure]]
|modes = [[Single player]]
|ratings = [[Entertainment Software Rating Board|ESRB]]: Teen (13+)
|platforms = [[DOS]], [[Apple Macintosh|Mac]]
|media = [[CD-ROM|CD]] (1)
|requirements = 486DX/33MHz [[Central processing unit|CPU]], 8MB [[Random access memory|RAM]], 1MB [[Hard disk]] space, 256 colour [[Video Graphics Array|VGA]], [[DOS]] 5.0
|input = 
}}
'''''Full Throttle''''' is a graphical [[adventure game]], originally developed in-house and released in [[May]] [[1995]] by [[LucasArts]]. It is the tenth game to use the [[SCUMM]] adventure game engine.

== Plot ==
{{Spoiler}}

The story focuses on Ben, the leader of a biker gang called the Polecats. He is approached in a bar by Malcolm Corley, the CEO and founder of the last domestic motorcycle manufacturer in the country, Corley Motors. When he declines an offer, he is knocked out and thrown in a dumpster by the sinister vice president of Corley Motors, Adrian Ripburger, who seems to have designs on taking over the company. He attempts to link up with the Polecats, whom Ripburger has coerced into guarding Corley and himself to the annual shareholder's meeting.  Along the way, he meets a female mechanic named Mo, who later is revealed to be Corley's illegitimate daughter.  

Ben finds his gang in time to witness Ripburger murder Corley, and pin the murder on the Polecats.  A fugitive, he is forced to cross the desert to find a way to clear his name and save both his gang and prevent Ripburger from turning Corley Motors into a minivan producer. He crosses paths with a number of other motorcycle gangs, the speed addicted Vultures (of whom Mo used to be a member), the brutish Rottwheelers and the enigmatic Cavefish. He arrives at the shareholders meeting in time to catch Ripburger announcing his control over the company,  and he and Mo expose Ripburger as the murderer, and broadcast the last will and testament of Malcolm Corley, who names Mo as the rightful successor to his company.  Ben is able to free his gang, and the game ends with Ben and his gang riding off into the sunset. The game was admired for its polished look and involving storyline; its only major criticism was that it was of insufficient length.

[[Image:Full_Throttle_screenshot.PNG|left|thumb|235px|Full Throttle running in [[ScummVM]].]]

== Technical information ==
The game was released only on [[CD-ROM]], featuring a full voiceover soundtrack. The project was led by ''[[Day of the Tentacle]]'' creator [[Tim Schafer]], who was also its writer and designer, and featured the vocal talents of [[Roy Conrad]] (as Ben) and [[Mark Hamill]] (as Ripburger)&amp;mdash; known to be a consistent contributor to LucasArts' games. It was also one of the only LucasArts games to use externally recorded music, courtesy of the [[Gone Jackals]]. Certain tracks from their then current album, ''Bone to Pick'', were featured in the game.  

In [[2003]], development on the sequel '''''Full Throttle II: Hell on Wheels''''' began. A video teaser was released and an interactive demo was shown at [[E3]] that year, but shortly afterwards the game was cancelled.

== Voice cast ==
''Full Throttle'' employed plenty of skilled voice acting professionals, such as [[Kath Soucie]], [[Maurice LaMarche]], [[Tress MacNeille]], [[Hamilton Camp]] and Mark Hamill. ''Full Throttle'' was the first computer game to employ mostly [[Screen Actors Guild|SAG]]-registered professional voice actors instead of relying entirely on in-house talent, and also featured a few pieces of licensed music.

== See also ==
* [[LucasArts adventure games]]

== External links ==
