      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox CVG| title = The Dig
|image = [[Image:The Dig cover.jpg|270px|center|The Dig cover art]]
|developer = [[LucasArts]]
|publisher = [[LucasArts]]
|designer = [[Sean Clark]]
|engine = [[SCUMM]] v7
|released = [[1995]]
|genre = [[Adventure game|Adventure]]
|modes = [[Single player]]
|ratings = [[ESRB]]: Everyone (6+)
|platforms = [[DOS]], [[Apple Macintosh|Mac]]
|media = [[CD-ROM|CD]] (1)
|requirements = 486DX2/66MHz [[Central processing unit|CPU]], 8MB [[Random access memory|RAM]], 1MB [[Hard disk]] space, 256 color [[Video graphics array|VGA]], [[DOS]] 6.0
|input = 
}}

'''''The Dig''''' is a graphical [[adventure game]] developed by [[LucasArts]], and released  in [[1995]]. It was the 11th game to use the [[SCUMM]] game engine, and is notorious for its [[Steven Spielberg]] relation, and prolonged production that had the game bordering on [[vaporware]].

It is the single most serious LucasArts adventure game, containing absolutely no [[slapstick]] humor or even any subtle jokes found in all other LucasArts adventure titles. It is also the only one that perfectly fits in the [[science fiction]] genre.

==Story==
{{Spoiler}}

''The Dig'' starts with a [[radio telescope]] in [[Borneo]] picking up an unidentified object, directly heading towards [[Earth]]. It is a giant [[asteroid]] that would cause catastrophic damage upon impact. A team of five is made to go up into [[Space Shuttle Atlantis]] to stabilize the asteroid (which is named ''Attila'' after [[Attila the Hun]]) in Earth's orbit. The five members of this crew are:

* '''Boston Low''', the main [[protagonist]] and a retired astronaut and survival expert (voiced by [[Robert Patrick]])
* '''Dr. Ludger Brink''', an [[archaeologist]] and [[geologist]] (voiced by [[Steven Blum]])
* '''Maggie Robbins''', a reporter and fluent linguistics expert (voiced by [[Mari Weiss]])
* '''Ken Borden''', expert shuttle pilot (voiced by [[David Lodge (voice actor)|David Lodge]])
* '''Cora Miles''', [[NASA]] technician and political candidate (voiced by [[Leilani Jones]])

The mission was to plant small explosives on Attila to halt or alter its course. However, Low, Brink and Robbins find the asteroid to be hollow. After exploration (and activation) by the crew, it turns into a starship in the shape of a shining [[dodecahedron]], transporting the three to an alien, distant planet of unknown position. The crew starts to explore the deserted planet (now named [[Cocytus]] by Brink), their main priority being to find a way back home. Unfortunately, while the planet has a high state of technology, it is in an advanced state of decay and appears to be utterly devoid of [[sapience|sapient]] life.

The story's emphasis is on the stranded trio's interaction (and occasional conflict) as they spread out to explore the strange world, each in their own way: the commanding Low determined to find the way to go home, the precocious and stubborn linguist Maggie Robbins studying the alien civilization, and the geologist Ludger Brink, who seems to be sliding into a state of hostile [[obsession]].
{{Endspoiler}}

The strongest carrying element of the game is often considered to be the sense of wonder coming from the exploration of a seemingly-abandoned alien planet, backed by the soundtrack and graphical art. While the story, based on the interaction of the crew and carried mainly through dialogue, is also widely present, complaints are levelled at its serious atmosphere, uninteresting characters and over-reliance on somewhat heavy-handed dialogue. Some of the more alien puzzles were also met with frustration by the players.

==Overview==
[[Image:The dig.png|left|thumb|250px|A screenshot of ''The Dig'' running in [[ScummVM]].]]

The Dig was originally conceived by Spielberg as an episode of [[Amazing Stories (television)|Amazing Stories]] (and later as a [[film]]), but was concluded prohibitively expensive &amp;mdash; and difficult. During the game's release, the director did not deny the possibility of making it into a movie, but ten years later nothing to that effect has happened, and any future filming of the story seems highly doubtful.

The game has a legendarily heavy-hitting credit list for a computer game: The project leader was LucasArts grand [[Sean Clark]], [[Industrial Light and Magic]] created some of the CG imagery, it is based on a story idea by [[Steven Spielberg]] and has writing credits for Spielberg, famed author [[Orson Scott Card]] (who wrote the dialogue), and the well-known [[interactive fiction]] author [[Brian Moriarty]] (whose previous Lucas engagement was with [[LOOM]]).

Released as [[CD-ROM]] only, The Dig was sold for [[IBM PC compatible|PC]] and [[Apple Macintosh|Macintosh]] computers. It contains a full voice-over soundtrack and a digital orchestral score. For the most part, the game's graphics are hand-drawn and sparsely animated, with a mixture of pre-rendered 3D and hand-drawn animation clips also presented in certain parts of the game.

The music (composed by [[Michael Land]]) consisted of overtures and excerpts from the work of [[Richard Wagner|Wagner]]. It fit the company's iMUSE ideology (not to mention the game's scenery) perfectly with its ambient, dynamic flow. Land cited the music he personally composed for The Dig as the type closest to his own individual style. The Dig was also the first Lucas game to have its soundtrack also sold separately as an audio CD, adapted as a linear continuity of finite pieces. The CD was released in small numbers, however, and this rarity lead a lot of gamers to desperately look for it. With the popularity of the [[Internet]], the tracks themselves can be found more easily, although with potentially dubious legality.

[[Alan Dean Foster]] also wrote a [[novel]] based on the game. This novel is not completely consistent with the game but it is interesting in that it presents the point of view of the aliens, something not seen in the game. The novel also provides some background detail (such as the reaction on Earth after the discovery of Attila), in addition to filling several plot holes and in-game inexplicabilities.

==Notoriety==
The Dig had by far the longest development length of all LucasArts adventure games. Originally started in the [[1980s]], the game was designed by three distinct teams in succession, after the previous team gave up [http://home.comcast.net/~ervind/digstuff/digtext1.html]. Each design cycle abandoned some aspects and embraced others  from the earlier designs, and the team that finally put the game together and delivered the release admitted to a somewhat rushed, desperate construction, with the end result being an arguably fragmented and ropey game experience, with inconsistencies abound. Even the three main graphical elements of the game - the hand-drawn art for the alien world, the pre-rendered 3D graphics and the hand-drawn cel animation for cut-scenes are very different-looking. The game was, however, met favorably by the press and gamers alike upon release.

Notable omissions of previous design ideas through the game's production include a [[Survival skills|survival]] angle, which forced you to keep water and food supplies for life support, exploration of entire huge cities on the planet, and an extra character; a [[Japanese people|Japanese]] science-hobbyist business tycoon named Toshi Olema, who funded the Attila project as long as he was a part of the crew.

==External links==
