'''''Born Yesterday''''' is a play written and first directed by [[Garson Kanin]] and adapted into a successful [[1950 in film|1950]] film. It was later remade again in [[1993 in film|1993]] with the same name. Actress and comedienne [[Judy Holliday]] originated the role of Billie Dawn on [[Broadway theatre|Broadway]], as well as the [[George Cukor]] adaptation made by [[Columbia Pictures]]. The remake, directed by [[Luis Mandoki]] and released through [[Buena Vista Pictures]], starred [[Melanie Griffith]] as the central character and updated the plot.

== Plot ==
An uncouth, corrupt tycoon, Harry Brock, brings his showgirl mistress Billie Dawn with him to [[Washington, D.C.]] He also hires a journalist, Paul Verrall, to educate his girfriend. In the process of learning, Billie Dawn realizes how corrupt her boyfriend is and begins interfering with his plans to &quot;buy&quot; a few [[Congress|Congressmen]].

== 1946 stage production ==
The stage production of the play opened on February 4th, 1946 at the Lyceum Theater, where it began the first of nearly two thousand performances. Directed by its writer, [[Garson Kanin]], and starring [[Judy Holliday]] (Dawn), [[Paul Douglass]] (Brock), and [[Gary Merrill]] (Verrall), the play opened to rave reviews. After over two years at the Lyceum, the play moved to the larger Henry Miller Theater with the same cast, where it finished off its run on [[Christmas Eve]] of 1950.

=== Cast ===
[[Paul Douglas]] ''as'' Harry Brock&lt;br&gt;
[[Judy Holliday]] ''as'' Billie Dawn&lt;br&gt;
[[Gary Merrill]] ''as'' Paul Verrall&lt;br&gt;
[[Carroll Ashburn]] ''as'' The Assistant Manager&lt;br&gt;
[[Frank Otto]] ''as'' Eddie Brock&lt;br&gt;
[[Larry Oliver]] ''as'' Senator Norval Hedges&lt;br&gt;
[[Mona Bruns]] ''as'' Mrs. Hedges&lt;br&gt;
[[C.L. Burke]] ''as'' A Waiter&lt;br&gt;
[[Ellen Hall]] ''as'' Helen&lt;br&gt;

== 1950 film ==
The play was adapted into a [[1950]] comedy film, [[Born Yesterday (1950)]],  by Albert Mannheimer, which was directed by [[George Cukor]]. It stars [[Judy Holliday]], [[Broderick Crawford]] and [[William Holden]].

The movie won the [[Academy Award for Best Actress]] (Holliday) and was nominated for [[Academy Award for Costume Design|Best Costume Design, Black-and-White]], [[Academy Award for Directing|Best Director]], [[Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]] and [[Academy Award for Writing Adapted Screenplay|Best Writing, Screenplay]].

=== Cast ===
